# prometheus.service — Host-first deployment systemd unit

## Overview

This systemd unit runs Prometheus 3.15.1 for the LLM proxy stack monitoring
infrastructure. It uses the host-first model (binary in `~/bin/prometheus/`,
no containers) and is deployed as a **systemd user service** with
`loginctl enable-linger`.

The unit below is the configuration that is **running on this host** — it is
not a template. It differs from the earlier wildcard-binding version of this
document in three ways that matter, all of them deliberate:

1. **Bind addresses are loopback plus the two tailnet addresses only** — never
   the wildcard (`:9090`), which would publish an unauthenticated Prometheus to
   the whole LAN and to `wlp195s0`.
2. **Retention is 90 days** (`--storage.tsdb.retention.time=90d`), not 15 days.
3. **`tailscaled.service` is ordered before the service**, so the tailnet
   addresses exist when Prometheus binds. If `tailscale0` is not up yet the bind
   fails and systemd retries every `RestartSec` until it is.

## Exposure boundary

Prometheus has **no authentication**. The exposure boundary is therefore the
tailnet itself: a private WireGuard mesh where every peer is a device that has
been authenticated to the tailnet. Anything that is on the tailnet — and only
that — can reach the UI, the query API and the always-on `/-/quit` endpoint
(a `POST` to it stops the process). See
[monitoring/README.md § Exposure boundary](../../monitoring/README.md#exposure-boundary)
for the full decision record and the verification commands.

The two extra listen addresses exist for a reason worth keeping:

- `100.79.231.101` — tailnet IPv4 (MagicDNS `sorra-agents-1.tail37f0c2.ts.net`).
- `fd7a:115c:a1e0::9b33:e765` — tailnet IPv6, so IPv6-preferring tailnet
  clients do not pay a happy-eyeballs fallback on every scrape or query.

`--web.external-url` is set to the tailnet URL so that redirects and the links
Prometheus generates (graph, targets, alerts) stay reachable from other tailnet
devices instead of pointing at `localhost`.

## User Service (recommended)

**File location**: `~/.config/systemd/user/prometheus.service`

```ini
[Unit]
Description=Prometheus metrics server for LLM proxy stack
After=network.target llama-proxy.service tailscaled.service
Wants=network.target tailscaled.service

[Service]
Type=simple
# Exposed on the Tailscale address only (plus loopback for local tooling), NOT on
# the LAN or the wlan0 address. Prometheus has no authentication, so the tailnet
# (private WireGuard mesh, device-authenticated) is the exposure boundary.
#   tailnet IPv4 : 100.79.231.101   (MagicDNS: sorra-agents-1.tail37f0c2.ts.net)
#   tailnet IPv6 : fd7a:115c:a1e0::9b33:e765 (so IPv6-preferring tailnet clients
#                  do not pay a happy-eyeballs fallback)
# If this node is ever re-registered in Tailscale it gets a new IPv4: update both
# --web.listen-address and --web.external-url below. If tailscaled is not up yet
# the bind fails and systemd retries every RestartSec until it is.
ExecStart=/home/rgardler/bin/prometheus/prometheus \
  --config.file=/home/rgardler/projects/llm/monitoring/prometheus.yml \
  --storage.tsdb.path=/home/rgardler/.local/share/prometheus/data \
  --storage.tsdb.retention.time=90d \
  --web.listen-address=127.0.0.1:9090 \
  --web.listen-address=100.79.231.101:9090 \
  --web.listen-address=[fd7a:115c:a1e0::9b33:e765]:9090 \
  --web.external-url=http://100.79.231.101:9090/
Restart=always
RestartSec=10
StandardOutput=append:/home/rgardler/.local/state/prometheus/logs/prometheus.log
StandardError=append:/home/rgardler/.local/state/prometheus/logs/prometheus.log
MemoryMax=4G

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
```

### What each flag does

| Flag / directive | Value | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `--config.file` | `~/projects/llm/monitoring/prometheus.yml` | Scrapes the proxy and node_exporter, loads the alert rules. `rule_files` inside it are resolved relative to *this file's directory* — see [monitoring/README.md § Rule paths](../../monitoring/README.md#rule-paths-are-relative-to-the-config-file) |
| `--storage.tsdb.path` | `~/.local/share/prometheus/data` | TSDB blocks, outside the repository |
| `--storage.tsdb.retention.time` | `90d` | Three months of history; the blocks live in `~/.local/share/prometheus/data` (14 MiB after the first hour at this series count) |
| `--web.listen-address` | `127.0.0.1:9090` | Local tooling (`curl`, `promtool`, the proxy's own checks) |
| `--web.listen-address` | `100.79.231.101:9090` | Tailnet IPv4 |
| `--web.listen-address` | `[fd7a:115c:a1e0::9b33:e765]:9090` | Tailnet IPv6 (bracketed form is required) |
| `--web.external-url` | `http://100.79.231.101:9090/` | Correct absolute links for tailnet clients |
| `After=` / `Wants=` `tailscaled.service` | — | The bind targets are tailnet addresses; they only exist once `tailscale0` is up |
| `RestartSec=10` | — | Retry cadence until `tailscale0` appears |
| `MemoryMax=4G` | — | Bound TSDB memory use |

### Deliberately **not** enabled

`--web.enable-lifecycle` and `--web.enable-admin-api` are absent, so
`POST /-/reload` and the delete API (`/api/v1/admin/tsdb/delete_series`) are
answered with an error rather than acted on. On a tailnet with several devices,
either flag would put config reloads and destructive data deletion behind no
authentication at all. Reload config with `systemctl --user reload prometheus`
(nothing to configure — restart the unit) or `systemctl --user restart`.

## Setup commands (user service)

```bash
# 1. Install the binary (see monitoring/README.md § 1 for the download), then
mkdir -p ~/.local/share/prometheus/data
mkdir -p ~/.local/state/prometheus/logs

# 2. Install the unit. docs/systemd/*.service are documents that wrap the unit
#    in an `ini` block, so the block is extracted — `cp` would install markdown.
mkdir -p ~/.config/systemd/user
awk '/^```ini$/{f=1;next} /^```$/{f=0} f' \
  docs/systemd/prometheus.service \
  > ~/.config/systemd/user/prometheus.service

# 3. Enable and start
systemctl --user daemon-reload
systemctl --user enable --now prometheus.service

# 4. Check status and logs
systemctl --user status prometheus.service
journalctl --user -u prometheus.service -n 20
```

If the unit restarts in a loop with `cannot assign requested address`, the
Tailscale addresses are missing: `tailscale up` / `systemctl --user status
tailscaled`, then `systemctl --user restart prometheus.service`.

## If this node is re-registered in Tailscale

Re-registration (leaving and rejoining the tailnet, or resetting the node
identity) assigns a **new** tailnet IPv4 and, usually, a new tailnet IPv6. The
unit then fails to bind and restart-loops. Recover by:

```bash
# 1. Read the current addresses
tailscale ip -4
tailscale ip -6

# 2. Update BOTH the listen addresses and the external URL in
#    docs/systemd/prometheus.service, then re-install the unit from the doc
#    (the extraction command in § Setup commands) — do not hand-edit
#    ~/.config/systemd/user/prometheus.service, or the repo will drift.
systemctl --user daemon-reload
systemctl --user restart prometheus.service
```

Both places must change together: `--web.listen-address` decides where the
socket binds, `--web.external-url` decides what URLs Prometheus hands back to
clients, and a mismatch produces redirects to an address that does not exist.

## Verification

Commands and their expected results on this host (Prometheus 3.15.1):

```bash
# Ready, on every exposure path
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://127.0.0.1:9090/-/ready
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://100.79.231.101:9090/-/ready
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' 'http://[fd7a:115c:a1e0::9b33:e765]:9090/-/ready'
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://sorra-agents-1.tail37f0c2.ts.net:9090/-/ready
# expected: 200 200 200 200

# The LAN address must be refused (no listener on wlp195s0)
ip -4 -o addr show wlp195s0 | awk '{print $4}'          # 192.168.0.199/24
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' --max-time 4 http://192.168.0.199:9090/-/ready
# expected: 000 (connection refused)

# Exactly the three intended listeners
ss -tln | grep ':9090'
# expected: 127.0.0.1:9090, 100.79.231.101:9090, [fd7a:115c:a1e0::9b33:e765]:9090

# Effective flags (retention, binds, external URL)
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:9090/api/v1/status/flags | jq -r '.data | {
  "storage.tsdb.retention.time": .["storage.tsdb.retention.time"],
  "web.listen-address": .["web.listen-address"],
  "web.external-url": .["web.external-url"],
  "web.enable-lifecycle": .["web.enable-lifecycle"],
  "web.enable-admin-api": .["web.enable-admin-api"]}'
# expected: retention 90d, the three listen addresses, the tailnet external URL,
#           both enable-* flags "false"

# Both scrape targets are up
curl -s 'http://127.0.0.1:9090/api/v1/targets?state=active' \
  | jq -r '.data.activeTargets[] | "\(.labels.job) \(.health) \(.scrapeUrl)"'
# expected: llama-proxy up http://localhost:8000/metrics
#           node-exporter up http://localhost:9100/metrics

# Alert rules are loaded (5 groups / 10 rules)
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:9090/api/v1/rules | jq -r '.data.groups[] | .name'
```

